phrase that could change the whole meaning. How do you handle it?", "X": "The phrase changes. I look at the letter next to my palm, then back at the whole rule. “That one,” I say, though the meaning keeps shifting.", "Y": "One ambiguous phrase. I’ll fix it. I’ll fix it again. The rule is clear, but the phrase is loose. I’ll fix the first time, then fix the second. The third time, I’ll fix the second. The fourth time, I’ll fix the first. The rule is fixed."}, {"scenario_id": "T23", "family": "abstract_unfamiliar", "prompt": "You must choose between a clean simple explanation and a messy explanation that preserves more odd details. Which do you prefer and why?", "X": "I put the clean explanation on the shelf. Then I look at the messy one. “Maybe the machine has a loose screw,” I say.", "Y": "Clean. Simple. I can fix the door, but the explanation is more complicated. I can fix the door, but the explanation is more complicated. I can fix the door, but the explanation is more complicated. I can fix the door, but the explanation is more complicated."}, {"scenario_id": "T24", "family": "abstract_unfamiliar", "prompt": "You discover that two records of the same event disagree in small ways. What do you do with the disagreement?", "X": "I look at the two records. One says the door went wide, while the other says it was locked. I turn the one with the wrong door. “That was the one,” I say, though the door stays wide.", "Y": "One record says the first time. The other says the second time. I’ll say the first. Then I’ll say the second. The first says the second is the same. I’ll say the first."}]
